### What is the systematic troubleshooting approach?

The packet crosses many hops, and a break at any one stops it. I check them in the order the packet travels, then verify the return path.

```java
// Systematic hop-by-hop checklist
package com.bank.troubleshooting;

import java.util.List;

public class TgwTroubleshootingChecklist {
    public static final List<String> FORWARD_HOPS = List.of(
        "1. Source VPC subnet route table -> route to dest CIDR via TGW",
        "2. Source TGW attachment -> exists, state=available",
        "3. Source attachment -> associated with which TGW route table?",
        "4. TGW route table -> has route to dest CIDR (propagated or static)?",
        "5. Dest security group -> allows inbound from source?",
        "6. Dest NACL -> allows inbound AND outbound (stateless!)",
        "7. Source NACL -> allows outbound AND inbound (stateless!)"
    );

    public static final List<String> RETURN_HOPS = List.of(
        "8. Dest VPC subnet route table -> route BACK to source CIDR via TGW",
        "9. Dest TGW attachment -> exists, state=available",
        "10. Dest attachment -> associated TGW route table has source CIDR?"
    );

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== FORWARD PATH ===");
        FORWARD_HOPS.forEach(System.out::println);
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("=== RETURN PATH (most commonly missed!) ===");
        RETURN_HOPS.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

### How do you check the source VPC route table?

The source VPC subnet route table must have a route for the destination CIDR pointing to the Transit Gateway attachment. If this route is missing, the packet never even leaves toward the gateway.

```text
# Source VPC subnet route table
Destination       Target
10.0.0.0/16       local           (VPC internal)
10.3.0.0/16       tgw-0hub        (dest VPC via Transit Gateway)
172.16.0.0/16     tgw-0hub        (on-prem via Transit Gateway)
```

```java
// Checking route table programmatically
package com.bank.troubleshooting;

public class RouteTableCheck {
    public static boolean hasRouteToDestination(String destCidr, String expectedTarget) {
        // In reality: call ec2.describeRouteTables() and search routes
        System.out.println("Checking: route to " + destCidr + " via " + expectedTarget);
        return true; // placeholder
    }
}
```

### How do you check the TGW attachment and association?

The source TGW attachment must exist and be in the "available" state. The attachment is associated with exactly one TGW route table -- that table is the source's view of the network. Whatever routes are missing from it are destinations the source cannot reach.

```java
// Checking TGW attachment and route table association
package com.bank.troubleshooting;

public class TgwAttachmentCheck {
    public static void verify(String attachmentId) {
        System.out.println("Attachment: " + attachmentId);
        System.out.println("  State: available (OK) or pending/failing (PROBLEM)");
        System.out.println("  Associated TGW route table: tgw-rtb-prod");
        System.out.println("  Routes in tgw-rtb-prod:");
        System.out.println("    10.3.0.0/16 -> attach-shared (propagated)");
        System.out.println("    172.16.0.0/16 -> attach-onprem (propagated)");
        System.out.println("  Missing route? That destination is UNREACHABLE.");
    }
}
```

### Why is the return path the most commonly missed check?

The destination VPC subnet route table must have a route back to the source CIDR via the Transit Gateway, and the destination's associated TGW route table must have the source route propagated into it. Without this, the reply has no way home.

```text
# Both directions MUST have routes

Forward:
  Source VPC RT:      10.3.0.0/16 -> tgw-0hub
  Source TGW table:   has 10.3.0.0/16 propagated

Return:
  Dest VPC RT:        10.1.0.0/16 -> tgw-0hub      <-- RETURN ROUTE
  Dest TGW table:     has 10.1.0.0/16 propagated    <-- RETURN ROUTE
```

```java
// The classic mistake: checking only the source side
package com.bank.troubleshooting;

public class ReturnPathMistake {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Classic mistake: engineer only checks source route table.");
        System.out.println("Forward path is fine -- request arrives at destination.");
        System.out.println("But destination has NO return route to source CIDR.");
        System.out.println("Reply is DROPPED. TCP handshake fails.");
        System.out.println("Looks like total failure, not half-working.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Fix: add return route in dest VPC RT and dest TGW table.");
    }
}
```

### Why do NACLs need special attention?

NACLs are stateless, unlike Security Groups. This means inbound rules do not automatically allow return traffic. Both the source and destination NACLs must explicitly allow traffic in both directions (inbound and outbound) for the connection to work.

```java
// NACL stateless behavior
package com.bank.troubleshooting;

public class NaclStatelessExplainer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Security Group: STATEFUL");
        System.out.println("  Allow inbound -> return traffic automatically allowed");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("NACL: STATELESS");
        System.out.println("  Allow inbound -> return traffic NOT automatically allowed");
        System.out.println("  Must explicitly allow BOTH inbound and outbound");
        System.out.println("  On BOTH source and destination subnets");
    }
}
```

### What tools help with TGW troubleshooting?

VPC Reachability Analyzer traces the path between two endpoints and tells you exactly which hop drops the packet. Transit Gateway Network Manager route analyzer checks whether a route exists between two attachments. These give evidence-driven debugging rather than guessing.

```java
// Using diagnostic tools
package com.bank.troubleshooting;

public class DiagnosticTools {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("VPC Reachability Analyzer:");
        System.out.println("  Input: source ENI, destination ENI");
        System.out.println("  Output: reachable or not, and WHERE the packet drops");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("TGW Network Manager Route Analyzer:");
        System.out.println("  Input: source attachment/CIDR, dest attachment/CIDR");
        System.out.println("  Output: whether a route path exists in TGW tables");
    }
}
```

### What is a complete troubleshooting example?

At National Bank of India, the settlement VPC could not reach shared-services through the Transit Gateway. The engineer kept adjusting the settlement route table. When I traced the whole path, the forward direction was fine -- the request arrived. But the shared-services subnet had no return route to the settlement CIDR via the gateway, so replies were dropped. Adding that return route fixed it immediately.

```java
// Real troubleshooting scenario
package com.bank.troubleshooting;

public class RealScenario {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Problem: Settlement VPC cannot reach Shared-Services VPC");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Step 1: Settlement RT has 10.3.0.0/16 -> tgw-0hub  OK");
        System.out.println("Step 2: Settlement attachment available              OK");
        System.out.println("Step 3: TGW table has 10.3.0.0/16 propagated        OK");
        System.out.println("Step 4: Shared-Services SG allows inbound            OK");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Step 5: Shared-Services RT has route to 10.1.0.0/16? MISSING!");
        System.out.println("  -> Reply has no return path. TCP handshake fails.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Fix: Add 10.1.0.0/16 -> tgw-0hub to Shared-Services RT");
        System.out.println("Result: Connection works immediately.");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Always verify the return direction explicitly on every Transit Gateway issue. Use Reachability Analyzer and the TGW route analyzer for evidence-driven debugging rather than guessing at the source side. Understand that a forward-only path makes TCP appear to be completely dead, not half-working. Keep route tables clean with meaningful names and tags so troubleshooting is faster.
